\section{Compile Rules into Relations and Joins}

A bottom-up engine materializes facts rather than expanding an individual goal. A rule body is a join of the relations it names: repeated variables constrain columns to agree, constants select matching rows, and head variables specify the output columns. Recursion feeds new rows into subsequent joins. Consider this complete Souffl\'e program for paths in a toy compiler's dependency graph. Node names are constants, not terms describing whole paths.

\begin{verbatim}
.decl edge(src:symbol, dst:symbol)
.decl reach(src:symbol, dst:symbol)
.output reach

edge("a", "b").
edge("a", "c").
edge("b", "d").
edge("c", "d").
edge("d", "e").

reach(x, y) :- edge(x, y).
reach(x, z) :- reach(x, y), edge(y, z).
\end{verbatim}

The recursive rule joins on \texttt{y} and projects \texttt{(x,z)}. The first rule seeds nonempty paths of length one. A naive round joins the entire current \texttt{reach} relation with \texttt{edge} and unions the result back in. Once a pair was tested against a stable \texttt{edge} relation, doing exactly the same join on that pair next round cannot produce a new result. Semi-naive evaluation exploits that fact without changing the least model; Abiteboul, Hull, and Vianu treat this distinction between database semantics and evaluation \cite{abiteboul1995foundations}.

