\section{Walkthrough: Extend Only the Newly Discovered Paths}

The pseudocode is for this linear recursion, with one occurrence of \texttt{reach} in the recursive body. It uses sets throughout. Build a lookup table on the source column of the fixed relation \texttt{edge}; it maps \texttt{d}, for instance, to the successor set \(\{\texttt{e}\}\).

\begin{verbatim}
known := set(edge)
delta := known
while delta is not empty:
    proposed := empty set
    for (x, y) in delta:
        for z in successors[y]:          # join on the middle node
            proposed.add((x, z))        # coalesce repeated witnesses
    next := proposed minus known        # never schedule an old pair
    known := known union next
    delta := next
return known
\end{verbatim}

At loop entry \texttt{delta} consists exactly of the pairs first inserted in the preceding stage (or the seed); every older pair has already been joined with all its successors. Joining the five seed pairs emits four witnesses: two for \texttt{(a,d)}, one for \texttt{(b,e)}, and one for \texttt{(c,e)}. Only three pairs enter the next delta. Their join proposes just \texttt{(a,e)}, because the other two pairs end at \texttt{e}, which has no successors. The next join proposes nothing. The final table has nine rows. \Cref{fig:visual-344} shows what is scheduled, not every tuple already in memory.

