At termination every stored pair has had all of its extensions considered. Thus the result is closed under both rules. Only facts supported by a rule enter the result, so by induction it is contained in every model. Together these properties identify it as the least model, not a table of all proofs. This argument applies even if a cycle creates infinitely many paths: the finite set of pairs bounds the number of insertions.

\section{Multiple Recursive Inputs Need Multiple Delta Variants}

For a recursive rule with two occurrences of the same relation, write \(R_{\mathrm{new}} = R_{\mathrm{old}} \cup \Delta\), with \(R_{\mathrm{old}}\) the accumulated rows before the most recent insertion. Joining only \(\Delta\) with \(\Delta\) misses pairs where a new row matches an older row. On the other hand, the join of two old inputs has already been considered. A disjoint split of input combinations is \(\Delta \mathbin{\operatorname{join}} R_{\mathrm{new}}\) together with \(R_{\mathrm{old}}\mathbin{\operatorname{join}}\Delta\), using the rule's join constraints. Projection can still produce duplicate heads, so subtract the accumulated head relation and deduplicate before scheduling. In a mutually recursive component, use the old, new, and delta tables for each body occurrence of a predicate defined in that component. Stable inputs do not need a delta variant.

For example, replace the recursive rule above by \verb|reach(x, z) :- reach(x, y), reach(y, z).|. After joining seed pairs, \texttt{(a,d)} is new but \texttt{(d,e)} is old. Their combination belongs in the first variant. Likewise \texttt{(a,b)} is old and \texttt{(b,e)} is new, so the second variant can independently derive the same \texttt{(a,e)} tuple. Taking the first changed body occurrence partitions the join work; it does not partition the possible output tuples.

\section{Keep the Delta Separate from the Storage Strategy}

The fixed-point connection is due to van Emden and Kowalski's account of definite clauses. The efficiency argument is closely related to the change-driven work lists in Khedker, Sanyal, and Karkare's \emph{Data Flow Analysis: Theory and Practice}: propagate a change only to places where it can matter. A production implementation chooses indexes and join order from the rule's equality keys and relation sizes. The quadratic number of possible stored pairs still applies; semi-naive evaluation avoids old-input work but does not make the closure small. A useful test compares output sets against naive saturation on a graph with converging paths and a cycle. Applying the algorithm to deletions requires something more: removing one edge need not invalidate a reachable pair if another path still supports it.

The compiler-facing result is the closure, not the chosen delta schedule. Keep the schedule and its indexes behind the analysis interface so that a client such as a dependency checker sees stable tuples while the implementation changes its join plan.
